---
import Legal from '../../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { site } from '../../site';
---

<Legal
  lang="en"
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="How Trakta and trakta.app handle personal data: the app collects nothing, everything stays on your phone."
  alternate="/integritet/"
>
  <div class="summary">
    <p>
      <strong>In short:</strong> Trakta collects no personal data. Everything you enter in the app stays on your phone,
      and nobody else – me included – can see it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    Trakta is developed as open source by {site.author} as a private individual. For questions about this policy or your
    data, reach me at <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The app</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The app has no internet access, no account and no sign-in.</li>
    <li>
      Everything you enter – trips, drives, commuting, notes and documents – is stored only on your phone. The database is
      encrypted, and documents are kept in the app's own private storage.
    </li>
    <li>Nothing is sent to me or to anyone else. The app contains no analytics, ads, tracking or crash reporting.</li>
    <li>
      The app does not ask for access to your files. When you add a document you pick the file yourself in the phone's
      file picker, and the app keeps a copy of that one file.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you create a backup, for example to switch phones, it is an encrypted file protected by a password you choose. You
      decide where the file is saved, for example Google Drive, iCloud Drive or an email to yourself, and that service's
      terms apply there. Without the password nobody can open the file, including me.
    </li>
    <li>If you remove the app, all its data is deleted. There is no copy anywhere else, except backups you saved yourself.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Downloads and GitHub</h2>
  <p>
    Trakta's source code is on GitHub, and the Android app is downloaded from there. The download button on trakta.app
    is a plain link that takes you to GitHub. GitHub is owned by Microsoft and processes technical data such as your IP
    address when you download the app, under
    <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="noopener">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.
    The same applies if you let an app such as Obtainium fetch updates from GitHub. I can see how many times the app has
    been downloaded, but not who downloaded it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The test version in Google Play is handled by Google under Google's terms and privacy policy. If you take part in the
    test, I have the email address you gave to join it and use it only for the test. Apart from that I only receive
    aggregated statistics from Google Play, such as the number of installs, but nothing that identifies you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Issues and discussions on GitHub are public. Do not post personal data or details from your tax return there. For
    anything private, email me at <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> instead.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    trakta.app is hosted by Cloudflare. Like any web server, Cloudflare processes technical data such
    as your IP address to deliver the site and protect it from abuse. The site uses no cookies and no analytics, and loads
    nothing from third parties.
  </p>

  <h2>If you support Trakta</h2>
  <p>
    The "buy me a coffee" button is a plain link to Buy Me a Coffee; this website loads nothing from them. If you choose
    to support Trakta, the payment happens at Buy Me a Coffee and its payment partners under their terms and privacy
    policy. I see the name, email address and message you choose to share there, and use them only to thank you.
  </p>

  <h2>When you email me</h2>
  <p>
    If you write to me, I use your email address and what you write to reply. I keep it no longer than needed to handle
    your message. The legal basis is my legitimate interest in answering questions and bug reports.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Under the GDPR you have the right to know what data I hold about you, to have it corrected or deleted, and to object
    to its processing. As the app collects nothing, I normally hold no data about you at all apart from any email you
    have sent and, if you take part in the Google Play test, your email address there. Contact me at <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>. You can also complain to the Swedish
    Authority for Privacy Protection, <a href="https://www.imy.se/en/" rel="noopener">imy.se</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If the app or this website changes in a way that affects your data, I will update this policy and the date at the
    top of the page.
  </p>
</Legal>
